/* ============================================================================
   rule-of-72.epher -- the Rule of 72 estimate of doubling time versus the
   exact doubling time, for rates from 6% to 10%

   The Rule of 72 says money doubles in about 72/(100*r) years at annual
   rate r, i.e. 72 divided by the rate in percent: 12 years at 6%. The exact
   doubling time solves (1+r)^t = 2, so t = ln(2)/ln(1+r). This script prints
   both side by side for every whole percent from 6 to 10. Source: standard
   finance lore; the exact formula is Kellison, The Theory of Interest,
   chapter 2. The rule stays within a few months of the truth across this
   range.

   Accuracy: the exact times are irrational and print to 12 digits; 72/7
   prints as the fraction 72/7 because its decimal repeats (epher's exact
   fraction display shows the same number as 10.2857142857...).

   Run it from a terminal:
     epher "epher scripts/finance/interest/rule-of-72.epher"

   The rate range 6% to 10% is the rule's usual comfort zone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- helpers ----------------------------------------------------------------
def doubletime(r) = ln(2) / ln(1 + r)   // exact doubling time at rate r, years

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Table rows are: rate in percent, the rule's estimate 72/rate, and the
// exact doubling time ln(2)/ln(1 + rate/100).
for p in 6 to 10 do {p, 72 / p, doubletime(p / 100)}
// The classic example: at 6% the rule says 12 years, the exact answer is
// 11.8957, so the rule runs about 0.10 years (5 weeks) high.
print("rule of 72 at 6%:", 72 / 6, "years")
print("exact doubling time at 6%:", doubletime(6%))
/* ---- expected output ----
= {{6, 12, 11.8956610459}, {7, 72/7, 10.2447683511}, {8, 9, 9.006468342}, {9, 8, 8.04323172693}, {10, 7.2, 7.27254089734}}
= rule of 72 at 6%: 12 years
= exact doubling time at 6%: 11.8956610459
*/
